# Runtime state: SQLite, extracted SDK, prepared Python environments, the serve
# record and its log. Everything under here is machine-local and rebuildable.
.otter/

# Integration identity markers. The runtime writes one per integration
# directory; it names *this checkout's* instance, not the example, so a clone
# mints its own. A committed marker is replaced with a different id on the first
# run, which shows up as an unexplained modification in a repo nobody edited.
.otter-id
.otter-id.tmp-*

# Secrets: never commit an env file, templates included.
#
# There is deliberately no `!*.env.example` re-include here. Git cannot tell a
# filled-in template from a placeholder, and a `.env.example` that someone
# pasted real values into is a secret like any other.
#
#   otter.env    the daemon's credentials, at the repository root
#   .env, *.env  read by nothing; ignored so a stray one cannot be committed
#   .env.*       .env.example, .env.local, .env.production
#   *.env.*      otter.env.example, otter.env.production
#
# otter.env.example is tracked deliberately, and stays tracked: ignore rules do
# not apply to a file git already tracks. These rules only stop a *new* template
# from being added by accident, which is why one has to be added with
# `git add -f`. That is the point.
#
# These are path rules: they cannot tell a filled-in template from a
# placeholder. Keep real values out of any file you intend to commit, and use
# a content-level backstop (gitleaks, trufflehog) for the rest.
.env
.env.*
*.env
*.env.*

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
